## 3. User Stories and Acceptance Criteria

### Overview
**User Stories** are short, simple descriptions of functionality told from the perspective of the end user. They translate epics into actionable development tasks.

### Structure
> As a [type of user], I want [goal] so that [benefit].

### Example
- As a user, I want to filter search results by price so that I can find affordable products faster.

### Acceptance Criteria
Acceptance criteria define **the conditions that must be met** for a story to be considered complete. They clarify expectations between PMs, designers, and engineers.

**Example Criteria:**
- User can apply filters for price range and see updated results.  
- Filters persist when navigating between pages.  
- No performance lag beyond 2 seconds.

### PM Insight
Good user stories are **independent, testable, and valuable**. Acceptance criteria prevent scope creep and miscommunication during sprints.

